WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.300 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  you may have heard of the million dollar  Banksy painting that was shredded at  auction right after it's sold now art  experts think that it could be worth  nearly double but why how is the value  of any work of art determined will be  honest it seems a little bit arbitrary  and was this the destruction of a  valuable piece of art or the creation of  a new one  [Music]  on October 5th 2018 Sotheby's in London  auctioned off a framed girl with balloon  painting the Banksy piece was valued at  approximately two to three hundred  thousand pounds at the auction the  paintings sold for eight hundred sixty  thousand pounds plus Sotheby's  commission making the total an  impressive 1 million in 42,000 pounds  that's about 1.4 million dollars as the  hammer fell an alarm sounded from the  frame and the painting spontaneously  shredded after the option Banksy took to  Instagram to reveal that he himself had  built the shredder into the frame this  type of stunt is pretty typical Banksy  but apparently this one did not go  exactly as planned  nearly two weeks later bangs he posted a  second video showing that the shredder  had malfunctioned  he claimed that it was supposed to cut  through with the entire piece instead of  stopping halfway  [Music]  so what makes this such a historical  moment in the art world we asked  Danielle ROM the managing director of  New York fine art appraisers and senior  member of the American Society of  appraisers the stunts that banks he  pulled at the auction was brilliant in  both conception and execution the most  captivating thing about this story is  how easy it is to understand it has  become really this viral talking point  for everybody about the meaning of art  and the meaning of money in capitalism  and it's become such a larger issue than  just the painting itself being shredded  the piece has since been renamed love is  in the bin but how much is this piece  actually worth now there's been a lot of  talk about whether he actually destroyed  art or created it and I'm in the school  that he created it it was essentially  performance art in in execution and then  on top of that performance art it made a  new conceptual piece of art and even got  a new title love is in the bin is  definitely one of Banksy's most iconic  images at this point there's been a  tremendous amount of media coverage  about it that certainly self perpetuates  and makes it worth even more than it  would have been anyway  but it's burned in everybody's mind who  has seen that video the moment where it  shreds through the through the picture  frame is not going anywhere I would say  that if this was to go up for auction  again it would bring significantly more  than it did the first time possibly over  twice the amount and how is the value of  a piece determined Danielle told us that  art appraisers try to examine each piece  in person they look at the physical  condition to see how much it's worth  then they research the piece and  compared to similar works that have sold  recently in order to place a value on it  since the news broke art collectors have  flocked to art brokers asking whether or  not they should shred their own  authentic Banksy pieces  according to my art broker calm website  that resells Banksy artwork one person  even went through with it and shredded  an unsigned mint Banksy print worth  40,000 pounds hoping it would then be  worth double  however the destruction of the piece  allegedly reduced its value to only one  single pound if you own a Banksy print  my recommendation is not to shred it  Banksy as an artist has the right to  disavow any work that's been damaged  altered or mutilated beyond this  original attack so he can shred the work  and he can create a new work out of it  but someone else trying it at home is  not gonna have as much luck the person  who was the successful bidder on this  Banksy piece lucked out not only did she  get the bang seat but she got the Banksy  she got the you know the media hype  around her piece it's definitely gonna  sell for more down the line and I think  that when it's hanging in her wall and  someone walks in they'll know exactly  what it is whenever that painting  resurfaces again it's going to be an  exciting moment  [Music]

